  "result": "I've checked two sources and saved my notes, so here's my answer.\n\n**Neither: start with one agent.** More agents don't always give better results. It's a tempting idea because more people often get more done, and sometimes extra agents do help.\n\nTake \"Is the laser cutter free Friday? If so, book it.\" Split that across a calendar agent and a booking agent, and the first has to pass its findings to the second. If it passes \"Friday is free\" but drops \"only after 2 pm\", the booking can go wrong. It's like relaying a dinner reservation through several friends.\n\nA **multi-agent system** is several agents dividing up a job. In [Anthropic's research system](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system), a team of agents beat a single agent by 90.2% on an internal research test. But Anthropic says such systems suit work with many parts that can run in parallel, and are a poor fit when agents must share the same information or depend heavily on each other.\n\nCost matters for your budget. A **token** is a small chunk of text, and model usage is typically billed by the token. In Anthropic's data, single agents used about 4 times as many tokens as a chat, and multi-agent systems about 15 times.\n\n**What to do:** design one agent with a few tools (search manuals, check the calendar, book time). Test it on about 20 realistic student questions. Add complexity only [when it measurably helps](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents). For your design review: \"We'll split only if tests show a failure that splitting fixes.\"\n\n**Your turn:** a teammate says, \"Booking is important, so it should get its own agent.\" How would you reply, in one or two sentences?\n\nI can also turn this into a short lesson, though context and memory is still the next topic I'd suggest.",
